Telco Business Requirement Document 

 1 Business Challenges
The data highlights three critical challenges that are currently costing the business revenue:
Contractual Volatility: A massive portion of the customer base is on Month-to-month contracts. This creates a "leaky bucket" where revenue is unpredictable and customer loyalty is non-existent.
Fiber Optic Reputation: Fiber Optic is usually a premium product, yet its churn rate is high (~42%). If your premium product has the lowest retention, the business is effectively losing its highest-paying customers.
The Digital Gap: The high churn among Senior Citizens suggests that the current billing (Paperless) or payment methods (Electronic Check) might be creating friction for this demographic.
2. Key Outcomes (Descriptive Insights)
Based on the data, we have identified several "Red Flag" patterns that define why customers are leaving:
The "Price Gap": Customers who churn pay an average of $74.44/month, while retained customers pay $61.27/month. This $13 difference suggests that high-tier pricing is driving customers toward competitors.
The "Crucial 18-Month" Mark: The average tenure of a customer who leaves is only 18 months, compared to 38 months for those who stay. The first year and a half of the customer journey is your highest-risk period.
Support & Security as Retention Anchors: Customers who do not have Online Security or Tech Support churn at a staggering rate of ~41%. When these services are added, churn drops to ~15%.
Segment Risks: * Senior Citizens churn at nearly double the rate of younger demographics (41.7% vs 23.6%).
Fiber Optic users have a much higher churn rate than DSL users, indicating potential service reliability issues or price dissatisfaction.
3. Predictions (Predictive Analytics)
By moving this data into a system like Microsoft Fabric, we can build models to forecast:
Churn Probability Score: Every current customer can be assigned a "Risk Score" (0 to 100%). We can predict who is likely to leave before they call to cancel.
Customer Lifetime Value (CLV): We can predict the total revenue a customer will generate over their entire relationship, allowing the business to decide how much they should spend to "save" a specific customer.
Next Best Offer: Based on the data that shows Tech Support reduces churn, the model can predict which customers should be offered a free trial of "Support Services" to lock them in.
4. Strategic Recommendations
To move the needle on retention, I recommend the following three-pillar strategy:
Pillar A: The "Retention Bundle" Strategy
Action: Bundle "Online Security" and "Tech Support" into the standard Internet packages.
Reasoning: The data shows that these services are the strongest "glue" keeping customers loyal. Even offering them for free for 6 months can double the retention rate for new customers.
Pillar B: Migration Incentives
Action: Launch a campaign to migrate "Month-to-month" customers to "1-Year Contracts" by offering a small discount (e.g., $5 off) that brings their monthly charge closer to the $61 "loyalty average."
Reasoning: Customers on 2-year contracts almost never churn (~3% rate).
Pillar C: Targeted Senior Citizen Support
Action: Introduce a specialized support line or non-digital billing options for the 65+ demographic.
Reasoning: Addressing the 41% churn rate in this specific segment could save millions in annual recurring revenue (ARR)


