Strategy :
1. Categorical Simplification (Reducing Noise)
In your dataset, columns like OnlineSecurity have three values: "Yes", "No", and "No internet service".
The Problem: To a computer, "No" and "No internet service" are different.
The Fix: We should consolidate "No internet service" into "No". This reduces noise and simplifies the patterns the AI has to learn.
2. Binary Encoding (1s and 0s)
The Problem: Power BI and ML models prefer numbers. If we want to calculate the "Percentage of users with Tech Support," it's hard to do with text.
The Fix: Convert all "Yes/No" columns into 1 and 0. This allows you to simply "Sum" a column in Power BI to get the total count of users.
3. Dropping Unique Identifiers
The Problem: customerID is a random string. If you include it in a machine learning model, the model might try to find patterns in the ID (which is impossible), leading to "Overfitting."
The Fix: We keep the ID in the "Analytics" table but remove it for the "ML-Ready" table.
