1. Why are they facing these issues? (The Root Cause)
The data we analyzed suggests a Reactive Culture rather than a Proactive Culture.
The "Silo" Effect: Marketing is likely focused on "New Customer" acquisition (cheap intro rates), while Customer Support is focused on "Ticket Closing." Neither is focused on Lifetime Value (LTV).
Feature Complexity: The fact that "Online Security" and "Tech Support" increase loyalty suggests that customers struggle with the technical side of the product. When they can't set it up easily, they quit.
The "Price-Only" Competition: By competing on monthly charges alone, they've attracted "Switchers"—customers who move as soon as a competitor offers a $5 discount.

2. Analytical Business Approach: The "Growth Flywheel"
Instead of just preventing churn, the business needs to transition to a Data-Driven Growth Model.
A. Propensity-to-Buy (Cross-Sell) Modeling
The Strategy: We already predicted who will leave. Now, use Python to predict who is most likely to upgrade.
Action: Look for "Low Risk" customers with high usage but low-tier plans. Offer them a "Speed Boost" for 3 months.
Scaling Impact: Increasing the Average Revenue Per User (ARPU) is often cheaper than finding a new customer.
B. The "Referral Loop" Strategy
The Strategy: Turn your "Low Risk / Long Tenure" customers into your sales force.
Action: Use your Python list to find "Promoters" (Tenure > 2 years, 0 Support tickets). Send them a referral code: "Refer a neighbor, and both get $20 off."
Scaling Impact: Referral leads typically have a 37% higher retention rate than traditional leads.
C. Reducing "Time-to-Value" (TTV)
The Strategy: Speed up the moment the customer feels "This is great!"
Action: If a customer signs up for Fiber, the employee (Installer) shouldn't just plug in the wires. They should be incentivized to set up the customer's Streaming App or Home Security on their phone before leaving.
Scaling Impact: First-month churn is the most expensive. High TTV kills that risk.

3. What can the Employees do?
Scaling isn't just for the C-Suite; it happens on the front lines.
Customer Support (The "Insight" Agents): Instead of just solving a bill issue, every agent should see the Churn Probability Score (from our ML model) on their screen.
If Score > 70%: The agent is authorized to offer a "Loyalty Credit" immediately without asking a manager.
The Data Team (Your Role): You move from "Report Generator" to "Strategy Designer."
Example: Automate an alert that pings the sales team when a "High Value" customer's usage drops by 30% (a leading indicator of churn).
Product Teams: Use the data to simplify. If customers who don't use "Streaming" are leaving, the product team should bundle a free Netflix or Disney+ sub to make the service "un-cancelable."

4. The Scaling Framework (The 80/20 Rule)
To scale the business 10x, apply the 80/20 Principle to your model:
Find the 20% of customers who provide 80% of your profit.
Study their "DNA": Do they all have Fiber? Do they all have 2-year contracts?
Clone them: Shift your entire marketing budget to only target people who look like that 20%.
Analytical Summary: Stop trying to save everyone. Use the ML model to identify the "Saveable High-Value" customers and ignore the "Chronic Switchers" who cost more to keep than they are worth.
Instead of looking for who is leaving, we are looking for your "Golden Cohort"—the customers who spend the most, stay the longest, and have the lowest risk.
By identifying the "DNA" of these customers, the marketing team can stop guessing and start targeting people who actually provide long-term profit.
